⟦300⟧If <math>\rho</math> represent the length of the normal B′M′ or the radius of curvature of the curve of buoyancy at an angle <math>\theta</math>, then <math>\rho.d\theta = ds</math> the length of an element of arc of the B curve. In the limit when <math>d\theta</math> is indefinitely small, <math>\frac{ds}{d\theta} = \rho</math>. Using Cartesian co-ordinates with B as origin and B<math>y</math>, B<math>z</math>, as horizontal and vertical axes, we have—
{{block center|<math>\frac{dy}{d\theta} = \frac{ds}{d\theta}\cos\theta = \rho\cos\theta,</math> . . . . (1)}}
{{block center|<math>\frac{dz}{d\theta} = \frac{ds}{d\theta}\sin\theta = \rho\sin\theta;</math> . . . . (2)}}
whence
{{block center|<math>y = \int_0^\theta \rho.\cos\theta.d\theta;\ z = \int_0^\theta \rho.\sin\theta.d\theta,</math>}}
and the righting lever <math>\text{GZ} = y\cos\theta + (z - \text{BG})\sin\theta</math>.

⟦500⟧The radius <math>\rho</math> is (as for the upright position) equal to the moment of inertia of the corresponding water-plane about a longitudinal axis through its centre of gravity divided by the volume of displacement; the integration may be directly performed in the case of bodies of simple geometrical form, while a convenient method of approximation such as Simpson’s Rules is employed with vessels of the usual ship-shaped type. As an example in the case of a box, or a ship with upright sides in the neighbourhood of the water-line, if <math>\text{BG} = a</math> and <math>\text{BM} = \rho_0</math>, then <math>\rho = \rho_0\sec^3\theta</math>; whence
{{block center|<math>y = \int_0^\theta \rho\cos\theta.d\theta = \rho_0\tan\theta,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>z = \int_0^\theta \rho\sin\theta.d\theta = \tfrac{1}{2}\rho_0\tan^2\theta,</math>}}
and
{{block center|<math>\text{GZ} = (\rho_0 - a)\sin\theta + \tfrac{1}{2}\rho_0\tan^2\theta.\sin\theta;</math>}}
which relations will also hold for a prismatic vessel of parabolic section. It is interesting to note that in these cases if the stability for infinitely small inclinations is neutral, ''i.e.'' if <math>\rho_0 = a</math>, the vessel is stable for small finite inclinations, the righting lever varying approximately as the cube of the angle of heel.

⟦1200⟧The application of the preceding formulae to actual ships is troublesome and laborious on account of the necessity for finding by trial the positions of the inclined water-lines which cut off a constant volume of displacement. To avoid this difficulty the process was modified by Reech and Risbec in the following manner:—Multiply equations (1) and (2) by <math>\text{V}.d\theta</math>, V being the volume of displacement; we then have—
{{block center|<math>d(\text{V}y) = \text{I}\cos\theta.d\theta,</math> . . . . (3)}}
{{block center|<math>d(\text{V}z) = \text{I}\sin\theta.d\theta,</math> . . . . (4)}}
where I is the moment of inertia of the inclined water-line about a longitudinal axis passing through its centre of gravity. These formulae have been obtained on the supposition that the volume V is constant while <math>\theta</math> is varying; but by regarding the above equations as representing the moments of transference horizontally and vertically due to the wedges, it is evident that V may be allowed to vary in any manner provided that the moment of inertia I is taken about the longitudinal axis passing through the intersection of consecutive water-lines. In particular the water-lines may all be drawn through the point of intersection of the upright water-line with the middle line, and the moments of inertia are then equal to <math>\tfrac{1}{3}\int r^3dx</math> for both sides of the ship, <math>r</math> being the half-breadth along the inclined water-line; the increase in volume is the difference between the quantity <math>\int d\theta \int \tfrac{1}{2}r^2dx</math> for the two sides of the ship.

⟦2400⟧If <math>\text{V}_\alpha</math>, <math>\text{V}_0</math> be the volumes of displacement at angles <math>\alpha</math> and <math>{}_0</math> respectively,
{{block center|<math>\text{V}_\alpha - \text{V}_0 = \int_0^\alpha d\theta \left[\int \underset{\text{difference}}{\tfrac{1}{2}r^2dx}\right],</math> . . . (5)}}
and substituting in (3) and (4) and integrating,
{{block center|<math>\text{V}_\alpha y = \int_0 d\theta \left[\int \underset{\text{sum}}{\tfrac{1}{3}r^3dx}\right]\cos\theta,</math> . . . (6)}}
{{block center|<math>\text{V}_\alpha z = \int_0^\alpha d\theta \left[\int \underset{\text{sum}}{\tfrac{1}{3}r^3dx}\right]\sin\theta.</math> . . . (7)}}

⟦2700⟧On eliminating <math>\text{V}_\alpha</math> in (5), (6) and (7), <math>y</math> and <math>z</math> can be found.

⟦2800⟧This is repeated at different draughts, and thus <math>\text{V}_\alpha</math>, <math>y</math> and <math>z</math> are determined at a number of draughts at the same angle, enabling curves of <math>y</math> and <math>z</math> to be drawn at various constant angles with V for an abscissa; from these, curves may be obtained for <math>y</math> and <math>z</math> with the angle <math>\alpha</math> as abscissa for various constant displacements; GZ being equal to
{{block center|<math>y\cos\alpha + (z - a)\sin\alpha.</math>}}

